    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity and EPS CAGRs are unusable: FY2022 EPS/equity carry the 8.6bn Sky goodwill impairment and FY2025 NI (20.0bn) includes large one-off gains/tax items, while buybacks of 7-13bn/yr shrink equity. Owner-FCF CAGRs (16.6% 3y) are an artefact of FY2025's 755m cash tax (OBBBA). Revenue 3y CAGR (0.6%) is the honest organic anchor; equity CAGR diverges >30% from both revenue and EPS.",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF series (OCF - PP&E - software - SBC): FY2020 11,910; FY2021 15,774; FY2022 11,310; FY2023 11,720; FY2024 11,255; FY2025 17,947. Flat at ~11.3-11.7bn for FY2022-24 despite 36bn of buybacks lifting it per share. SBC grew roughly in line with revenue, so SBC-adjusted and reported FCF CAGRs are close (16.6% vs 15.0% 3y)."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Broadband keeps losing subscribers to fibre overbuild and T-Mobile/Verizon fixed wireless and domestic broadband revenue falls ~4-5% a year, but converged wireless (record 448k lines in Q2-26), business services, Epic Universe and a now-profitable Peacock roughly offset it: revenue ~1% a year ex-events, pre-SBC EBITDA margin slips from ~30% to 29%, capex stays ~11% of revenue, and cash taxes normalise from the OBBBA trough to 23%. The mid-2027 NBCU/Sky spin is value-neutral in aggregate.",
    "bull": "Broadband losses peak in 2026-27 as 'irrational' sub-$40 gigabit pricing rationalises and the convergence bundle (wireless penetration of the ~31m broadband base) stems churn; Peacock scales from breakeven to a 1-2bn EBITDA contributor; Epic Universe sustains Orlando attendance; the separation sharpens capital allocation and removes the conglomerate discount. Revenue grows 2-2.5%, margin expands to 31.5%, capex intensity eases to 10.5%.",
    "bear": "Broadband enters a full repricing cycle: fibre passings grow 4-5% a year and FWA undercuts on price, subscriber losses worsen (KeyBanc ~665k in 2027) and ARPU falls, so the ~70%-margin connectivity profit pool shrinks year after year. Comcast must keep capex near 11.5-12% to match fibre speeds, theme-park attendance softness persists, linear advertising erodes, and separation adds dis-synergies. Revenue declines 1-3% a year and pre-SBC EBITDA margin compresses to 24% - a harvest business capitalised at 8x with -1% terminal growth."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Unlevered owner-FCF DCF on a pro forma ex-Versant base, charging SBC once (flows expensed, count flat), with capitalised software counted as capex and cash taxes normalised from the OBBBA trough. The market at ~8-9x EV/owner-FCF is pricing a long harvest of a shrinking broadband franchise; the model asks how bad that decline must be to justify today's price.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Broadband subscriber and ARPU decline steeper than the bear path (fibre + FWA repricing)",
      "Revenue base is an estimate: Versant FY2025 revenue (~6.8bn) and EBITDA (~2.4bn) are not restated in the filings",
      "Cash tax normalisation pace under OBBBA full expensing (each 5pp of cash tax ~ 1bn/yr of FCF)",
      "Separation dis-synergies, one-off costs and leverage allocation between the two companies (mid-2027)",
      "Book value of debt used in the bridge; long-dated low-coupon bonds trade well below par, which would raise equity value on a market-value bridge",
      "Theme-park attendance softness and advertising cyclicality"
    ]
  },
